{"catalog_digest":"ab192fe31d2a0b2b3ca40831d81ef8d274a6ab40b92001b0a52135e13db74d23","generation":"ab192fe31d2a0b2b3ca40831d81ef8d274a6ab40b92001b0a52135e13db74d23","tools":[{"capabilities":["magic-context:notes/v1"],"description":"Session notes are pending intentions: work you intend to return to, with its findings attached.\n\nUse notes for:\n- A finding to revisit when you return to the intended work\n- A decision with its reasoning, when follow-up work remains\n- A backlog item with evidence already found\n- Something the user explicitly asks you to note\n\nDon't use notes for: the next few steps; a plan you are actively executing; restart/fold insurance; or a record of how things stand (world-state, a design at a point in time) with nothing you intend to do about it — that goes stale silently; a fact worth keeping is memory, the rest is nothing. Use todos for active work. If the detail already lives in a file, record the path and what to inspect — don't copy the file into a note. Durable project facts belong in ctx_memory, not notes.\n\nFirst line is the title (under 80 chars), followed by detail. Operations:\n- write: save a new note (content required)\n- read: one row per note — `#id · age · title` — ready smart notes first, then newest; rows untouched 30+ days are marked stale. Pass note_ids to read full bodies; limit/offset page; filter selects other statuses.\n- update: change one note (note_ids=[N])\n- dismiss: retire 1–50 notes (note_ids). Dismiss a note when its work lands or is abandoned; a queue you never dismiss from stops being read.\n- surface_condition: make it a smart note — an outside checker periodically tests the condition using only externally verifiable signals (GitHub state, files, git, releases, web), never this conversation or future actions; the note is parked until the condition holds.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"action":{"description":"write | read | update | dismiss.","enum":["write","read","dismiss","update"],"type":"string"},"content":{"description":"Note text for write/update: first line is the title (under 80 chars), then the detail.","type":"string"},"filter":{"description":"Read filter: all, active, pending (unsurfaced smart notes), ready, dismissed. Omitted, it shows active session notes plus every current smart note (pending included); active shows only notes whose stored status is active.","enum":["all","active","pending","ready","dismissed"],"type":"string"},"limit":{"description":"Rows per read (default 25).","type":"number"},"note_ids":{"description":"Note ids: one for update, 1–50 for dismiss, or 1–50 to read those notes in full. Write and a plain read take an empty list.","items":{"maximum":9007199254740991,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"maxItems":50,"type":"array"},"offset":{"description":"Skip this many newest rows (default 0).","type":"number"},"surface_condition":{"description":"Makes this a smart note: a condition an outside checker can verify on its own, periodically — repository state, releases, web pages, anything it can look up — never something only this conversation knows. The note is parked until the condition holds.","type":"string"}},"required":["action","content","note_ids"],"type":"object"},"name":"ctx_note","result_ops":["prepend","append"],"schema_digest":"cf84d0fffd0c46b40b50c6297d8d02f73ef5e0527f66cd14e24718ce1e3ef4f4","semantics":1},{"capabilities":["magic-context:memory.write/v1"],"description":"Durable facts about this project, shared with every agent working on it and kept for the months this work lasts.\n\nYour active memories are already in <project-memory> as `#id: fact` lines. Write one when you learn something that must not have to be found again — a project rule, an architectural fact, a hard-won constraint, a config value, a naming convention — and especially when it cost you turns to find. One standalone fact per memory, phrased to make sense on its own. A pending intention with its evidence (\"do X later, here is what we know\") is ctx_note, not memory.\n\nActions:\n- write: new memory (content + category).\n- update: rewrite one memory whose fact changed (ids: [one], content; category optional to recategorize).\n- archive: retire wrong or obsolete memories (ids: [one or more], optional reason).\n- merge: collapse duplicates into one (ids: [two or more], content).\n- get: fetch by id (ids: 1–20), readable in every status.\nExamples: category=\"CONFIG_VALUES\", content=\"OpenCode source is at ~/Work/OSS/opencode\" · category=\"CONSTRAINTS\", content=\"Dashboard Tauri build needs RGBA PNGs, not grayscale\"","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"action":{"description":"write | update | archive | merge | get","enum":["write","update","archive","merge","get"],"type":"string"},"category":{"description":"Kind of fact (required for write; on update/merge, pass the memory's current category to keep it).","enum":["PROJECT_RULES","ARCHITECTURE","CONSTRAINTS","CONFIG_VALUES","NAMING"],"type":"string"},"content":{"description":"The memory text — one standalone fact (write, update, merge).","type":"string"},"ids":{"description":"Memory ids from <project-memory>: one for update, one or more for archive, two or more for merge, 1–20 for get.","items":{"type":"number"},"type":"array"},"reason":{"description":"Why it is being archived (optional).","type":"string"}},"required":["action","content","category","ids"],"type":"object"},"name":"ctx_memory","result_ops":["prepend","append"],"schema_digest":"de59b47f087c33bb84d20fe9eec0f5cdd5692089aa43e4bf19b72dcb53292cbc","semantics":1},{"capabilities":["magic-context:archive.search/v1"],"description":"Search this project's available memories, stored conversation, commits, primers and session notes. Results are plain search hits: this session has no compaction views, so results are not filtered against what is currently visible.\n\nPhrase query as a natural-language question carrying exact terms you expect in the answer (paths, symbols, config keys, error strings), for example \"why did we choose SQLite over postgres?\" A bare keyword stack finds less. A query containing only memory ids (`#7234`, `12, 34`) resolves them directly.\n\nSources (omit for all): memory (rules, constraints, conventions), message (stored conversation), git_commit (commit history), primer (reusable project Q&A), note (recorded follow-ups). Use from/to to restrict every source to an inclusive UTC date range. History recovery tools require active compaction and are not available in this tool set.","input_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"from":{"description":"Earliest date, YYYY-MM-DD (inclusive).","type":"string"},"limit":{"description":"Maximum results (default 10).","type":"number"},"query":{"description":"A natural-language question carrying the exact terms you expect in the answer.","type":"string"},"sources":{"description":"Restrict to these sources; omitting it or passing [] searches every source.","items":{"enum":["memory","message","git_commit","primer","note"],"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"to":{"description":"Latest date, YYYY-MM-DD (inclusive; default open).","type":"string"}},"type":"object"},"name":"ctx_search","result_ops":["prepend","append"],"schema_digest":"36ed2de214be00f8a0a281dc7e6bb3da815b9da5c2a233b9c9006f148ee11561","semantics":1}]}